.TH "tum\-borrow" "1" "" "" "tmux\-borrow Manual"
.SS NAME
\f[B]tum\-borrow\f[R] \[em] attach a utility or layout beside the host
pane
.SS SYNOPSIS
\f[B]tum borrow\f[R] [\f[I]util\f[R]|\f[I]layout\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]tum borrow\f[R] \f[B]\-h\f[R] | \f[B]\[en]help\f[R]
.SS DESCRIPTION
Attach a utility (or layout) instance beside the current host pane,
creating it first if none exists to reuse.
Borrowing moves a pane with \f[CR]join\-pane\f[R]/\f[CR]move\-pane\f[R]
\[em] the process is moved, not restarted, so its state is preserved.
.PP
Default\-mode resolution order: (1) an instance already bound to this
host is re\-shown; (2) else a hidden instance of this utility is
borrowed (the chooser picks if several \[em] pool instances are always
available, owned instances only to their home host); (3) else a new
\f[B]pool\f[R] instance is created and borrowed.
An orphan whose host is dead counts as hidden here; re\-borrowing clears
the dead binding.
.PP
With no argument, the chooser opens over every utility and layout.
Picking an instantiated one borrows that instance; picking an
uninstantiated one creates and borrows it.
Borrowing a layout borrows all its members into the host window at their
declared positions.
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\[en]help\f[R] Print a short synopsis (the SYNOPSIS
and EXAMPLES sections of this page) and exit.
For bare \f[B]tum\f[R], list every subcommand.
.SS EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
tum borrow htop          # reuse a hidden instance, else create a pool one
tum borrow               # chooser over all utilities and layouts
tum borrow dev           # borrow the \[aq]dev\[aq] layout (all members attach)
.EE
